Several people have posted complaints about being unable to recover
crashed nethack games.  If you are on UNIX, here's a hint which may
sometimes help.  It has enabled me to recover several good characters
who ran into bugs.

When a modem line drops for a session dialed into UNIX, a SIGHUP signal
is sent to processes on the port, to inform them that the line dropped.
Not all UNIX systems will do this, but most do. Usually if your UNIX
doesn't and you check closely, you'll find that it's local configuration
problems: perhaps the modem hardware isn't running all the lines thru to the
CPU, or perhaps the default stty() setting disables SIGHUP.  Get your
sysOp to fix it, in these cases.

Anyway, many UNIX programs are sensitive to SIGHUP and react sensibly
to it.  For example, vi will save your file under a scratch name and
e-mail you instructions on getting the file back.  NetHack is also
sensitive to SIGHUP and will save your file when it gets one.
This is nice if, like me, you have a poor quality dial-up line; if the
line drops, the game gets saved for you instead of lost.

If you have a hanging nethack session, you can try a variation on this.
Get to a different window/tty/etc., use ps to look up the
process number for the nethack session, and try "kill -HUP <pid>"
where <pid> is the number you just looked up.  If your version of
kill doesn't like "-HUP", look up the man pages for signal() (or sigsys())
and find the actual number for SIGHUP -- I think it's 1, but no promises.
Use the number instead (e.g., "kill -1 <pid>").

If nethack is not totally trashed, this will cause the hanging session
to save the character and exit, after which you can try again.

If you cannot get to a different window/tty/etc (say because you're
dialed in like I often am), then reach over and turn your modem off
instead, without killing the nethack session or anything.  This will
generally trigger the SIGHUP same as if you manually did a kill.  Dial in
again, and use ps to check if the nethack is still hanging.  If so,
try the manual method above; if not, hopefully your game is saved.

This won't rescue you from core dumping bugs, but it will save you
from e.g. infinite loops.

Good luck!
 
